Ashton <ashton@my-deja.com> writes:

> If I get a somewhat viable character going who advances to about level 
> 10 experience, that's the best the character is ever going to get.  At 
> 10, or maybe 11, the character has (for example) good armor, a nice 
> weapon or two, some useful magical items....  From that point on, traps, 
> rust monsters, succubus in whatever plurals, and various other monsters 
> and misadventures tend to drain items, levels, enchantments, abilities, 
> and what-have-you.

Much, though not quite all, of the damage you describe is reversible.
Have you discovered the wonders of the unicorn horn yet?  It will
restore any lost stats.  A drained level can be recovered by killing
one monster.  Are you losing lots of levels at once?  If so, do you
know which items provide good magical cancellation (which protects
against a lot of attacks including level drain)?

> I don't have a specific question, but the closest concerns traps, I 
> suppose.  Not only do I tend to lose potions, scrolls and armor 
> effectiveness to fire and water, but I also seem to lose 2 pets per game 
> to those, and falling rocks and pits and rolling boulders.  Do people 
> typically spend time searching before taking each step?  (Even my 
> rangers don't find enough of the traps in advance.)

Someone already mentioned that keeping your potions and scrolls in a
bag will protect them from fire.  Weapons and armour can be
rustproofed or fireproofed by reading a noncursed scroll of enchant
weapon/armour while confused - this will also repair any damage they
have already suffered.  When you mention water damage, are you just
talking about rust traps, or falling into water too?  If you're
falling into water, don't do that :-)  Grease your bag in case you do,
or use an oilskin sack.

Don't worry too much about losing pets - you can always tame more.
Keeping a pet alive for the entire game is quite a hard challenge.
